{{Interlanguage link|Oyama Shrine (Mount Tate)|lt=Oyama Shrine|ja|雄山神社|simple}} (雄山神社) is a [[Shinto shrine]] located in Japan that worships [[Mount Tate]]. The shrine is located at a high altitude of 3,003 meters and has three sub-shrines.<ref>https://zekkeijapan.com/spot/index/1147/</ref>

Oyama Shrine has a rich history and was historically a major site of {{ill|Shugendō|en}}, a Japanese religion that combines elements of Buddhism, Shintoism, and other indigenous beliefs.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Oyama Jinja - Toyama|url=https://en.japantravel.com/toyama/oyama-jinja/2595|access-date=2023-04-07|website=JapanTravel|language=en}}</ref>

The shrine claims to be the Ichinomiya (highest ranked Shinto shrine) of [[Etchū Province]], but there are other shrines that also claim this title.

Oyama Shrine is unique in that it does not have a honden, which is a main hall for enshrining the kami (deities), instead, [[Mount Tate]] itself is considered its main object of worship, known as the [[kannabi]].

== See Also ==
* Other shrines claiming to be Ecchu Ichinomiya
** [[Imizu Jinja]]
** [[Keta Jinja (Takaoka)]]
** [[Takase Shrine]]


