{{Interlanguage link|Oyama Shrine (Mount Tate)|lt=Oyama Shrine|ja|雄山神社|simple}} (雄山神社) is a [[Shinto shrine]] located in Japan that worships [[Mount Tate]]. The shrine is located at a high altitude of 3,003 meters and has three sub-shrines.<ref>https://zekkeijapan.com/spot/index/1147/</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=- Trip to Tateyama {{!}} Conference of Tateyama & Kurobe Alpine Route tourism area |url=http://www.tateyama-inc.jp/english/5_12.html |access-date=2023-09-15 |website=www.tateyama-inc.jp}}</ref>

It is located on Oyama peak, one of the three peaks of Mount Tate.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Fay |first=Tom |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=yuKPDwAAQBAJ&newbks=0&printsec=frontcover&pg=PT104&dq=Oyama+Shrine+(Tateyama)&hl=en |title=Hiking and Trekking in the Japan Alps and Mount Fuji: Northern, Central and Southern Alps |last2=Lang |first2=Wes |date=2019-03-27 |publisher=Cicerone Press Limited |isbn=978-1-78362-714-1 |language=en}}</ref>

It used to be called [[Tateyama]] [[Gongen]], but was renamed Oyama Shrine in 1869 during [[Haibutsu kishaku]].<ref name=":0">{{Cite book |last=Malá |first=Zuzana |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=XEjmDwAAQBAJ&newbks=0&printsec=frontcover&pg=PA74&dq=Oyama+Shrine+(Tateyama)&hl=en |title=Religious practices in the Japanese mountains: From fleeing the hells towards the healthy, sustainable and spiritual practices of the consumer society |date=2019-01-01 |publisher=Masarykova univerzita |isbn=978-80-210-9198-6 |language=en}}</ref>

Oyama Shrine has a rich history and was historically a major site of {{ill|Shugendō|en}}, a Japanese religion that combines elements of Buddhism, Shintoism, and other indigenous beliefs.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Oyama Jinja - Toyama|url=https://en.japantravel.com/toyama/oyama-jinja/2595|access-date=2023-04-07|website=JapanTravel|date=18 September 2012 |language=en}}</ref>.<ref name=":2">{{Cite book |last=Fukada |first=Kyūya |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=4VgEEAAAQBAJ&newbks=0&printsec=frontcover&pg=PT163&dq=Oyama+Shrine+(Tateyama)&hl=en |title=One Hundred Mountains of Japan |date=2014-12-31 |publisher=University of Hawaii Press |isbn=978-0-8248-4785-2 |language=en}}</ref>{{Rp|page=|pages=141-143}}

The shrine claims to be the Ichinomiya (highest ranked Shinto shrine) of [[Etchū Province]], but there are other shrines that also claim this title.

Oyama Shrine is unique in that it does not have a honden, which is a main hall for enshrining the kami (deities), instead, [[Mount Tate]] itself is considered its main object of worship, known as the [[kannabi]].

This is a rare practice and the only major shrines that continue the practice of direct [[Mountain Worship]] via [[Kannabi]] are [[Ōmiwa Shrine]] and [[Suwa-taisha]] and {{ill|Kanasana Shrine|simple|Kanasana Shrine|ja|金鑚神社}}.<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Cali |first1=Joseph |url=https://muse.jhu.edu/pub/5/monograph/book/21618 |title=Shinto Shrines: A Guide to the Sacred Sites of Japan's Ancient Religion |last2=Dougill |first2=John |date=2012 |publisher=University of Hawai'i Press |isbn=978-0-8248-3775-4 |location=Honolulu |pages=195}}</ref> <ref name="Nippon">{{Cite web |date=2018-07-08 |title=Keeping Ancient Mountain Worship Alive at Saitama's Kanasana Shrine |url=https://www.nippon.com/en/guide-to-japan/gu009002/keeping-ancient-mountain-worship-alive-at-saitama%E2%80%99s-kanasana-shrine.html |access-date=2023-10-18 |website=nippon.com |language=en}}</ref>


== History ==

=== Early History ===
The mountain's history goes back to the year 701. At that time, Saeki-no-Ariwaka's son, Ariyori, saw a vision of the [[Trikaya|Three Buddhas]] there. He then set up the Tateyama Daigongen Shrine.<ref name=":2" />{{Rp|page=|pages=141-143}}

=== Recognition ===
In 863, [[Emperor Seiwa]] recognized the mountain's god. This event is in the '[[Nihon Sandai Jitsuroku]]'.<ref name=":2" />{{Rp|page=|pages=141-143}}

It is recorded in the [[Engishiki Jinmyocho]] as a [[Shikinai Shosha]].

=== Literary References ===
Tateyama appears in old texts. It is sometimes mixed up with another mountain, [[Mount Tsurugi (Toyama)|Tsurugi]]. The mountain's deity is mentioned in these texts.<ref name=":2" />{{Rp|page=|pages=141-143}}

=== Religious Significance ===
Before the Meiji era, the mountain was a a major [[Shugendō]] pilgrimage site.<ref name=":2" />{{Rp|page=|pages=141-143}}

The shrine used to be called [[Tateyama]] [[Gongen]], but was forcibly renamed Oyama Shrine in 1869 during [[Haibutsu kishaku]] by [[Kaga Domain]] authorities.<ref name=":0" />

=== Modern Changes ===
Tateyama is now a tourist spot. It has a cable car and new roads. Plans include a tunnel for tourists. It has changed from a pilgrimage site to a tourist place. <ref name=":2" />{{Rp|page=|pages=141-143}}

== Mountain sites ==

=== Mountain Appearance ===
Tateyama looks like a continuous crest from a distance. Its peak is not clear. From Toyama, it is mostly hidden.<ref name=":2" />{{Rp|page=|pages=141-143}} The path to the top has many stories and monuments.<ref name=":2" />{{Rp|page=|pages=141-143}}

=== Log Basalt ===
There is basalt called Zaimoku-saka that looks like logs. According to legend a nun brought logs there for building Nyoindō and they were petrified.<ref name=":2" />{{Rp|page=|pages=141-143}}

=== Mirror Stone ===
The Mirror Stone is a highly reflective flat rock. It is about three yards wide. There is a legend about this stone. It involves Ariyori. Ariyori made the mountain accessible to pilgrims. His nurse followed him to this place. She could not go any further. She threw her mirror in desperation. The mirror turned into the Mirror Stone. There is another boulder nearby. It is known as the Old Woman Stone..<ref name=":2" />{{Rp|page=|pages=141-143}}

=== Jigoku-dani ===
This valley has steaming vents. It was seen as a hell-like place. It is in old tales and a Noh play.<ref name=":2" />{{Rp|page=|pages=141-143}} It not to be confused with the more famous [[Jigokudani Monkey Park]] where monkeys sit in hot springs.<ref name="readersnatural">{{Cite book |url=https://archive.org/details/naturalwondersof00sche |title=Natural Wonders of the World |publisher=Reader's Digest Association, Inc |year=1980 |isbn=0-89577-087-3 |editor-last=Scheffel |editor-first=Richard L. |location=United States of America |pages=[https://archive.org/details/naturalwondersof00sche/page/193 193] |editor-last2=Wernet |editor-first2=Susan J. |url-access=registration}}</ref>

=== Mikuri-ga-ike ===
This lake is known for its blue waters. A monk's story is linked to it.<ref name=":2" />{{Rp|page=|pages=141-143}}

People told the monk not to swim in the pool. But he refused to take their warnings and swam anyways.<ref name=":2" />{{Rp|page=|pages=141-143}}

The first time he swam, he had a dagger in his mouth. He came out safely. The second time, he swam without the dagger. He swam around once, then twice. On his third round, he was pulled under the water. He never came back up. This event named the lake "Thrice Round Pond." The name comes from his three swims in the lake..<ref name=":2" />{{Rp|page=|pages=141-143}}

=== Murodō ===
This is Japan's oldest mountain lodge. It was built in 1695.<ref name=":2" />{{Rp|page=|pages=141-143}}

== Architecture ==
Oyama Shrine has three parts.<ref name=":1" />{{Rp|page=|pages=97}} Similar to [[Chōkaisan Ōmonoimi Shrine]].<ref name=":22">{{cite web |title=chokaizan omonoimi shrine（warabioka kuchi no miya） - shrine-heritager |url=https://shrineheritager.com/en/chokaizan-omonoimi-shrine/ |url-status=unfit |archive-url=https://archive.li/XshvP |archive-date=2023-11-11 |access-date=2023-04-25 |website=shrineheritager.com}}</ref> The first part is the front altar. It is easy to reach. The second part is the prayer hall. It is at Ashikuraji, upriver from the altar. The third part is the main shrine. It is on the top of Mount Tateyama. The shrine is hard to reach. It is often covered in snow for half of the year.<ref name=":1">{{Cite book |last=Bandō |first=Masako |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=cstRDwAAQBAJ&newbks=0&printsec=frontcover&pg=PA97&dq=Oyama+Shrine+(Tateyama)&hl=en |title=Mandala Road |date=2013-02-01 |publisher=Anthem Press |isbn=978-0-85728-267-5 |language=en}}</ref>{{Rp|page=|pages=97}}

All three parts make up the Oyama Shrine. When the top is snowed in, people pray at the first two parts. These are nearer to the town. In the Edo Period, the area was busy with pilgrims. They often started their climb at the front altar. There were twenty-four lodges there for them. Ashikuraji had thirty-three lodges.<ref name=":1" />{{Rp|page=|pages=97}}

== Rituals ==

=== Cloth Bridge Rite ===
The Cloth Bridge rite is a very major rite of the Tateyama cult.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=|pages=75-79}}

=== Ritual Exchange of Robes ===
The meshikae is a yearly ritual on March 13th. It involves changing robes for Uba statues. It used to be for older women only. Now, more people can join. The robes are made by hand in a traditional way. They are brought to the Oyama Jinja shrine. There, they go through a procession and a cleaning ceremony..<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=|pages=75-79}}

=== Rituals and Media Attention ===
These rituals draw interest from researchers and the media. They show the mix of tradition, culture, and modern life. The changing of robes for the Uba statues gets a lot of media attention..<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=|pages=75-79}}

=== Yama no Kami Ritual ===
Another important ritual is for the [[Mountain God|yama no kami]], the god of [[Mount Tate]]. It takes place at the Shinpi Jinja. Local men and forestry workers gather for blessings.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=|pages=75-79}}

