==Gallery==
==== Upper Shrine ====
<gallery widths="200px" heights="150px">
File:Three mountains of Tateyama.jpg|'''Mount Tateyama'''<br>View of Tateyama from Murododaira (2450m). Tateyama consists of three peaks, with Fuji-no-Oritate on the left, Oyama in the middle, and Onanjiyama on the right. At the summit of Oyama, one can see the awarding place (large building) and the Mine-honsha Shrine (the part protruding like a turret).
File:Mine-honsha Gate.JPG|'''Mine-honsha Shrine Torii'''<br>Heading in the direction to the left of the image leads to the entrance of the route traversing towards Fuji-no-Oritate and Onanjiyama.
File:Mine-honsha Shaden.JPG|'''Mine-honsha Shrine Main Hall'''<br>The pebbles laid out are from pilgrims who picked up stones from riverbeds near their homes, wrote wishes on them, and brought them here for their prayers to be answered. The large flat stone in front of the right end of the shrine is the benchmark for Oyama at 3,003m. The orange robes to the left belong to priests who pray for the safety of climbers.
File:Mine-honsha Office.JPG|'''Mine-honsha Shrine Office (Shrine Office)'''<br>Viewed from in front of the shrine. The large building is the awarding place, and the small building on this side of the torii is the prayer reception. Beyond the awarding place and up a step is the Geographical Survey Institute first-order triangulation point "Tateyama".
File:Mine-honsha inside Office.JPG|'''Inside the Mine-honsha Shrine Office'''<br>If it's during the summer pilgrimage season, priests and shrine maidens are present, and they perform services like the distribution of protective charms.
File:Station of triangulation,Tateyama .JPG|'''Geographical Survey Institute First-Order Triangulation Point "Tateyama"'''<br>Located in front of the awarding place when ascending from Ichinokoshi. The cuboid stone marker is the "Tateyama" first-order triangulation point by the Geographical Survey Institute. Beyond it is a landscape indicator board, with directions engraved for Mount Fuji in Suruga and Mount Hakusan in Kaga.
File:Oyama-jinja Mine-honsha, old honden in Hotel Tateyama.jpg|'''Mine-honsha Shrine Old Main Hall'''<br>Built by Kaga Domain in 1860, dismantled in September 1995, and restored on July 1, 2013. Displayed inside Hotel Tateyama.
</gallery>

