{{Interlanguage link|Oyama Shrine (Mount Tate)|lt=Oyama Shrine|ja|雄山神社|simple}} (雄山神社) is a [[Shinto shrine]] located in Japan that worships [[Mount Tate]]. The shrine is located at a high altitude of 3,003 meters and has three sub-shrines.<ref>https://zekkeijapan.com/spot/index/1147/</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=- Trip to Tateyama {{!}} Conference of Tateyama & Kurobe Alpine Route tourism area |url=http://www.tateyama-inc.jp/english/5_12.html |access-date=2023-09-15 |website=www.tateyama-inc.jp}}</ref> Several million people visit the shrine every yearr.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Louis-Frédéric |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=p2QnPijAEmEC&newbks=0&printsec=frontcover&pg=PA953&dq=Oyama+Shrine+(Tateyama)&hl=en |title=Japan Encyclopedia |date=2002 |publisher=Harvard University Press |isbn=978-0-674-01753-5 |language=en}}</ref>

It is located on Oyama peak, one of the three peaks of Mount Tate.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Fay |first=Tom |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=yuKPDwAAQBAJ&newbks=0&printsec=frontcover&pg=PT104&dq=Oyama+Shrine+(Tateyama)&hl=en |title=Hiking and Trekking in the Japan Alps and Mount Fuji: Northern, Central and Southern Alps |last2=Lang |first2=Wes |date=2019-03-27 |publisher=Cicerone Press Limited |isbn=978-1-78362-714-1 |language=en}}</ref>

It used to be called [[Tateyama]] [[Gongen]], but was renamed Oyama Shrine in 1869 during [[Haibutsu kishaku]].<ref name=":0">{{Cite book |last=Malá |first=Zuzana |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=XEjmDwAAQBAJ&newbks=0&printsec=frontcover&pg=PA74&dq=Oyama+Shrine+(Tateyama)&hl=en |title=Religious practices in the Japanese mountains: From fleeing the hells towards the healthy, sustainable and spiritual practices of the consumer society |date=2019-01-01 |publisher=Masarykova univerzita |isbn=978-80-210-9198-6 |language=en}}</ref>

Oyama Shrine has a rich history and was historically a major site of {{ill|Shugendō|en}}, a Japanese religion that combines elements of Buddhism, Shintoism, and other indigenous beliefs..<ref name=":2">{{Cite book |last=Fukada |first=Kyūya |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=4VgEEAAAQBAJ&newbks=0&printsec=frontcover&pg=PT163&dq=Oyama+Shrine+(Tateyama)&hl=en |title=One Hundred Mountains of Japan |date=2014-12-31 |publisher=University of Hawaii Press |isbn=978-0-8248-4785-2 |language=en}}</ref>{{Rp|page=|pages=141-143}}.<ref name=":3">{{Cite book |last=Harding |first=Christopher |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=w0CDBAAAQBAJ&newbks=0&printsec=frontcover&pg=PA55&dq=Oyama+Shrine+(Tateyama)&hl=en |title=Religion and Psychotherapy in Modern Japan |last2=Fumiaki |first2=Iwata |last3=Shin’ichi |first3=Yoshinaga |date=2014-09-15 |publisher=Routledge |isbn=978-1-317-68300-1 |language=en}}</ref>{{Rp|page=|pages=55}}

The shrine claims to be the Ichinomiya (highest ranked Shinto shrine) of [[Etchū Province]], but there are other shrines that also claim this title.

Oyama Shrine is unique in that it does not have a honden, which is a main hall for enshrining the kami (deities), instead, [[Mount Tate]] itself is considered its main object of worship, known as the [[kannabi]].

This is a rare practice and the only major shrines that continue the practice of direct [[Mountain Worship]] via [[Kannabi]] are [[Ōmiwa Shrine]] and [[Suwa-taisha]] and {{ill|Kanasana Shrine|simple|Kanasana Shrine|ja|金鑚神社}}.<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Cali |first1=Joseph |url=https://muse.jhu.edu/pub/5/monograph/book/21618 |title=Shinto Shrines: A Guide to the Sacred Sites of Japan's Ancient Religion |last2=Dougill |first2=John |date=2012 |publisher=University of Hawai'i Press |isbn=978-0-8248-3775-4 |location=Honolulu |pages=195}}</ref> <ref name="Nippon">{{Cite web |date=2018-07-08 |title=Keeping Ancient Mountain Worship Alive at Saitama's Kanasana Shrine |url=https://www.nippon.com/en/guide-to-japan/gu009002/keeping-ancient-mountain-worship-alive-at-saitama%E2%80%99s-kanasana-shrine.html |access-date=2023-10-18 |website=nippon.com |language=en}}</ref>


