{{Interlanguage link|Oyama Shrine (Mount Tate)|lt=Oyama Shrine|ja|雄山神社|simple}} (雄山神社) is a [[Shinto shrine]] located in Japan that worships [[Mount Tate]]. The shrine is located at a high altitude of 3,003 meters and has three sub-shrines.<ref name=":1" />{{Rp|page=|pages=97}} Several million people visit the shrine every yearr.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Louis-Frédéric |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=p2QnPijAEmEC&newbks=0&printsec=frontcover&pg=PA953&dq=Oyama+Shrine+(Tateyama)&hl=en |title=Japan Encyclopedia |date=2002 |publisher=Harvard University Press |isbn=978-0-674-01753-5 |language=en}}</ref>

It is located on Oyama peak, one of the three peaks of Mount Tate.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Fay |first=Tom |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=yuKPDwAAQBAJ&newbks=0&printsec=frontcover&pg=PT104&dq=Oyama+Shrine+(Tateyama)&hl=en |title=Hiking and Trekking in the Japan Alps and Mount Fuji: Northern, Central and Southern Alps |last2=Lang |first2=Wes |date=2019-03-27 |publisher=Cicerone Press Limited |isbn=978-1-78362-714-1 |language=en}}</ref>

It used to be called [[Tateyama]] [[Gongen]], but was renamed Oyama Shrine in 1869 during [[Haibutsu kishaku]].<ref name=":0">{{Cite book |last=Malá |first=Zuzana |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=XEjmDwAAQBAJ&newbks=0&printsec=frontcover&pg=PA74&dq=Oyama+Shrine+(Tateyama)&hl=en |title=Religious practices in the Japanese mountains: From fleeing the hells towards the healthy, sustainable and spiritual practices of the consumer society |date=2019-01-01 |publisher=Masarykova univerzita |isbn=978-80-210-9198-6 |language=en}}</ref>

Oyama Shrine has a rich history and was historically a major site of {{ill|Shugendō|en}}, a Japanese religion that combines elements of Buddhism, Shintoism, and other indigenous beliefs..<ref name=":2">{{Cite book |last=Fukada |first=Kyūya |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=4VgEEAAAQBAJ&newbks=0&printsec=frontcover&pg=PT163&dq=Oyama+Shrine+(Tateyama)&hl=en |title=One Hundred Mountains of Japan |date=2014-12-31 |publisher=University of Hawaii Press |isbn=978-0-8248-4785-2 |language=en}}</ref>{{Rp|page=|pages=141-143}}.<ref name=":3">{{Cite book |last=Harding |first=Christopher |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=w0CDBAAAQBAJ&newbks=0&printsec=frontcover&pg=PA55&dq=Oyama+Shrine+(Tateyama)&hl=en |title=Religion and Psychotherapy in Modern Japan |last2=Fumiaki |first2=Iwata |last3=Shin’ichi |first3=Yoshinaga |date=2014-09-15 |publisher=Routledge |isbn=978-1-317-68300-1 |language=en}}</ref>{{Rp|page=|pages=55}}

The shrine claims to be the Ichinomiya (highest ranked Shinto shrine) of [[Etchū Province]], but there are other shrines that also claim this title.

Oyama Shrine is unique in that it does not have a honden, which is a main hall for enshrining the kami (deities), instead, [[Mount Tate]] itself is considered its main object of worship, known as the [[kannabi]].

This is a rare practice and the only major shrines that continue the practice of direct [[Mountain Worship]] via [[Kannabi]] are [[Ōmiwa Shrine]] and [[Suwa-taisha]] and {{ill|Kanasana Shrine|simple|Kanasana Shrine|ja|金鑚神社}}.<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Cali |first1=Joseph |url=https://muse.jhu.edu/pub/5/monograph/book/21618 |title=Shinto Shrines: A Guide to the Sacred Sites of Japan's Ancient Religion |last2=Dougill |first2=John |date=2012 |publisher=University of Hawai'i Press |isbn=978-0-8248-3775-4 |location=Honolulu |pages=195}}</ref> <ref name="Nippon">{{Cite web |date=2018-07-08 |title=Keeping Ancient Mountain Worship Alive at Saitama's Kanasana Shrine |url=https://www.nippon.com/en/guide-to-japan/gu009002/keeping-ancient-mountain-worship-alive-at-saitama%E2%80%99s-kanasana-shrine.html |access-date=2023-10-18 |website=nippon.com |language=en}}</ref>
== History ==

=== Early History ===
The mountain's history goes back to the year 701. At that time, Saeki-no-Ariwaka's son, Ariyori, saw a vision of the [[Trikaya|Three Buddhas]] there. He then set up the Tateyama Daigongen Shrine.<ref name=":2" />{{Rp|page=|pages=141-143}}

=== Recognition ===
In 863, [[Emperor Seiwa]] recognized the mountain's god. This event is in the '[[Nihon Sandai Jitsuroku]]'.<ref name=":2" />{{Rp|page=|pages=141-143}}

It is recorded in the [[Engishiki Jinmyocho]] as a [[Shikinai Shosha]].

=== Literary References ===
Tateyama appears in old texts. It is sometimes mixed up with another mountain, [[Mount Tsurugi (Toyama)|Tsurugi]]. The mountain's deity is mentioned in these texts.<ref name=":2" />{{Rp|page=|pages=141-143}}

=== Shugendo ===
Before the [[Meiji era]], the mountain was a a major [[Shugendō]] pilgrimage site.<ref name=":2" />{{Rp|page=|pages=141-143}}

The Tateyama cult is related to Mount Tateyama in Toyama Prefecture, Japan. This mountain is important in Shugendo. Tateyama has three peaks. Each peak is around 3,000 meters high. The cult started with [[mountain worship]]. It combines different beliefs. Oyama Shrine is at the mountain's summit. This shrine honors Shinto gods. It is also linked to a Buddhist paradise and hell.<ref name=":3" />{{Rp|page=|pages=55}} The cult is sometimes linked to the [[Kumano shrine|Kumano cult]].<ref name=":5">{{Cite book |last=Kaminishi |first=Ikumi |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=QAAuOkCJxHQC&newbks=0&printsec=frontcover&pg=PA170&dq=Oyama+Shrine+(Tateyama)&hl=en |title=Explaining Pictures: Buddhist Propaganda And Etoki Storytelling in Japan |date=2006-01-01 |publisher=University of Hawaii Press |isbn=978-0-8248-2697-0 |language=en}}</ref>{{Rp|page=|pages=220}}

Mount Tateyama is seen as a home to kami and the underworld. Buddhism influenced this view. Buddhist ideas of paradise and hell are found here. Many landmarks in Tateyama have names from Buddhism. For example, Mount Jodo is named after [[Amitābha|Amitābha Buddha's]] paradise. Dainichi Peak represents a major Buddhist figure. Lake Suzuri symbolizes a heavenly place in Buddhism...<ref name=":5" />{{Rp|page=|pages=167-173}}

Buddhist and Shinto beliefs [[Shinbutsu-shūgō|merged]] in Tateyama. A shrine on the mountain links [[Izanagi]] to [[Amitābha|Amitābha Buddha]]. Another peak connects [[Ame-no-Tajikarao]] a god of strength to the Buddhist god [[Acala]] (Fudo Myoo)...<ref name=":5" />{{Rp|page=|pages=167-173}}

Jigoku-dani (Hell Valley) is a place in Tateyama. It looks like a hell with boiling water and fire. People believed spirits of the dead were here. A Buddhist story talks about a condemned woman in this hell. Her sons tried to find her with a monk's help...<ref name=":5" />{{Rp|page=|pages=167-173}}

Japanese beliefs connect paradise and hell to our world. Buddhism teaches about good and bad actions. Good actions lead to a pure land. Bad actions lead to suffering in hell. Shamans tried to save people suffering in hell..<ref name=":5" />{{Rp|page=|pages=167-173}}

Buddhist ascetics were important in Tateyama. They belonged to a special Buddhist group. They trained in the mountains. They learned healing and other powers..<ref name=":5" />{{Rp|page=|pages=167-173}}

Tateyama attracted these priests. They believed valleys were mystical. They thought spirits protected their descendants from mountains..<ref name=":5" />{{Rp|page=|pages=167-173}}

Two communities, Iwakuraji and Ashikuraji, were the main centers of this cult.<ref name=":4">{{Cite book |last=Saka |first=Chihiro |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=9eB9EAAAQBAJ&newbks=0&printsec=frontcover&pg=PA137&dq=Oyama+Shrine+(Tateyama)&hl=en |title=Datsueba the Clothes Snatcher: The Evolution of a Japanese Folk Deity from Hell Figure to Popular Savior |date=2022-07-18 |publisher=BRILL |isbn=978-90-04-51767-7 |language=en}}</ref>{{Rp|page=|pages=137}} 

Iwakuraji was the predecessor to Maedate-shadan and Ashikuraji was the predecessor to Chuuguu-kiganden.<ref name=":4" />{{Rp|page=|pages=137}}

They both worshipped Tateyama Gongen. But they also competed for control over the mountains. Iwakuraji was at the mountain's base. This community guided pilgrims to Oyama Jinja Mine Honsha. They also worshipped Tachio Tenjin. Ashikuraji was up the foothills. They worshipped Ubason. They lost control battles to Iwakuraji. So, they focused on collecting contributions in cities. They also held rituals for those who couldn't climb the mountain.<ref name=":4" />{{Rp|page=|pages=137}}

Oyama Shrine is important in Tateyama. Two communities, Ashikuraji and Iwakuraji, were key. They practiced Buddhism and Shinto. They held services at Oyama Shrine. This shrine was recognized in old Japanese records..<ref name=":5" />{{Rp|page=|pages=167-173}}

In the 14th and 15th centuries, these communities were involved in politics. They also became known as healers. They helped with illnesses and other needs..<ref name=":5" />{{Rp|page=|pages=167-173}}

The communities relied on donations. They competed for the right to collect donations. In 1833, they agreed on their donation areas..<ref name=":5" />{{Rp|page=|pages=167-173}}

Missionaries from Ashikuraji traveled across Japan. They carried paintings. They promoted Tateyama mountain worship. They also held special rituals...<ref name=":5" />{{Rp|page=|pages=167-173}}

The Tateyama Mandala is a collection of paintings. They show stories and scenes from Tateyama. They are from the 17th century. The paintings vary in style. Some focus on stories. Others show landscapes or hell scenes. Owners paid for these paintings themselves..<ref name=":5" />{{Rp|page=|pages=167-173}}

Both communities were under the Kaga domain. They were different from other Shugen groups. They prayed for the domain's well-being. They organized events in their communities.<ref name=":4" />{{Rp|page=|pages=137}}

Tateyama Gongen is linked to [[Amitabha]] and [[Izanagi]]. [[Amitabha]] is a Buddhist figure. [[Izanagi]] is a [[Shinto]] kami. The worship of Tateyama Gongen may have started by the twelfth century.<ref name=":4" />{{Rp|page=|pages=137}}

The Tateyama cult became popular during the Edo period. This period was between the seventeenth and mid-nineteenth centuries. Both religious practitioners and ordinary people followed the cult. They came from all over Japan. They formed pilgrimage groups called Tateyama ko–. These groups visited the mountain from different regions. Villages grew along the pilgrimage route. They provided lodgings for the pilgrims.<ref name=":3" />{{Rp|page=|pages=55}}

At the base of Mount Tateyama, there are sacred sites. One of these is Nissekiji Temple in the Oiwa district. Sick people went there for healing. The temple was founded in 725. Buddhist monk Gyoki started it. He carved an image of Fudo Myo O, a Buddhist deity. This carving became significant in Shugendo. The temple was fully built by the mid-seventeenth century. Around 1700, people believed the temple's waterfalls could cure eye diseases. They also thought the waterfalls helped with mental illnesses.<ref name=":3" />{{Rp|page=|pages=55}}

=== Meiji Period ===
In the Meiji period, the worship sites on Mount Oyama changed. This was part of separating Buddhism from Shinto. The shrine at the peak became Oyama Jinja's inner shrine. It was named Mine Honsha. The former Iwakuraji and Ashikuraji became Maedachi Shaden and Kiganden.<ref name=":4" />{{Rp|page=|pages=137}}

The Kaga lord split Iwakuraji and Ashikuraji. This was to prevent them from becoming too powerful.<ref name=":4" />{{Rp|page=|pages=137}}

The shrine used to be called [[Tateyama]] [[Gongen]], but was forcibly renamed Oyama Shrine in 1869 during [[Haibutsu kishaku]] by [[Kaga Domain]] authorities.<ref name=":0" />

=== Modern Changes ===
Tateyama is now a tourist spot. It has a cable car and new roads. Plans include a tunnel for tourists. It has changed from a pilgrimage site to a tourist place. <ref name=":2" />{{Rp|page=|pages=141-143}}

