'''Oyama Shrine''' (おやまじんじゃ) is a {{ill|lt=Shinto shrine|Q845945|WD=Q845945|1=Shinto shrine}} located in {{ill|lt=Tateyama Town|Q670189|WD=Q670189|1=Tateyama, Toyama}}, {{ill|lt=Nakaniikawa District|Q1204336|WD=Q1204336|1=Nakaniikawa District, Toyama}}, {{ill|lt=Toyama Prefecture|Q132929|WD=Q132929|1=Toyama Prefecture}}. Its former names were '''{{ill|lt=Tateyama Gongen|Q11597837|WD=Q11597837|1=Tateyama Gongen}}''' and '''Oyama Gongen'''. It is a {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Shikinaisha|1=Shikinaisha|WD=Q134917286}}, the {{ill|lt=Ichinomiya|Q1656379|WD=Q1656379|1=Ichinomiya}} of {{ill|lt=Etchū Province|Q906899|WD=Q906899|1=Etchū Province}}. Under the {{ill|lt=Modern shrine ranking system|Q712534|WD=Q712534|1=Modern system of ranked Shinto shrines}}, it was classified as a {{ill|lt=Modern shrine ranking system#Government shrines|Q712534|WD=Q712534|lt=Kokuhei-shōsha|1=Modern system of ranked Shinto shrines}}, and today it is a {{ill|lt=Beppyo Shrine|Q10898274|WD=Q10898274|1=Beppyo shrine}} under the {{ill|lt=Association of Shinto Shrines|Q908077|WD=Q908077|1=Association of Shinto Shrines}}.

=== Overview ===
With the sacred peak {{ill|lt=Mount Tateyama|Q2977103|WD=Q2977103|1=Mount Tate}} as its {{ill|lt=shintai|Q327532|WD=Q327532|1=Shintai}}, the shrine enshrines two deities as gods of Tateyama: {{ill|lt=Izanagi|Q813858|WD=Q813858|lt=Izanagi-no-Ōkami|1=Izanagi}} (Tateyama Gongen Oyama no Kami, whose Buddhist honji is {{ill|lt=Amitābha Tathāgata|Q236242|WD=Q236242|1=Amitābha}}) and {{ill|lt=Tajikarao|UNKNOWN|lt=Ame-no-Tajikarao-no-Kami}} (Tachio Tenjin Tsurugi-dake no Kami, whose honji is {{ill|lt=Fudō Myōō|Q337624|WD=Q337624|1=Acala}}). During the era of {{ill|lt=shinbutsu-shūgō|Q2279088|WD=Q2279088|1=Shinbutsu-shūgō}}, it was a shrine with strong Buddhist elements and became the source of {{ill|lt=Tateyama Shugendō|Q11597829|WD=Q11597829|1=Tateyama Shugen}}. It also served as an imperial prayer site for {{ill|lt=Empress Genmei|Q233224|WD=Q233224|1=Empress Genmei}} and {{ill|lt=Emperor Go-Daigo|Q313942|WD=Q313942|1=Emperor Go-Daigo}}.

The shrine consists of three sanctuaries: '''{{ill|lt=#Mine-honsha|UNKNOWN|lt=Mine-honsha}}''' (みねほんしゃ), '''{{ill|lt=#Chūgū Kiganden|UNKNOWN|lt=Chūgū Kiganden}}''' (ちゅうぐうきがんでん), and '''{{ill|lt=#Maedate Shadan|UNKNOWN|lt=Maedate Shadan}}''' (まえだてしゃだん). These collectively are known as Oyama Shrine. They are located across a wide area from {{ill|lt=Ashikuraji|Q11614856|WD=Q11614856|1=Ashikuraji}} in {{ill|lt=Tateyama Town|Q670189|WD=Q670189|1=Tateyama, Toyama}} to Iwakuraji, broadly encompassing the entire Tateyama mountain range including Jigokudani and Midagahara.

There is also a small Oyama Shrine, a subsidiary of Tateyama, in {{ill|lt=Kamiichi Town|Q1204302|WD=Q1204302|1=Kamiichi, Toyama}}{{ill|lt=Araya|Q11501967|WD=Q11501967|1=新屋}}.

The character “'''峅'''” used in Iwakuraji and Ashikuraji is said to mean “a place where the gods descend.”

It is said that worship at any of the three shrines—Mine-honsha, Chūgū Kiganden, or Maedate Shadan—brings the same blessings. This is because Mine-honsha at the summit can only be visited from the 7th to 9th months of the lunar calendar; Chūgū Kiganden faces the main peak Oyama and is where the founder spent his later years; and Maedate Shadan is where the legend of the opening of Tateyama begins. In addition, it is said that in the past the {{ill|lt=chief priest|Q11453500|WD=Q11453500|1=Gūji}} walked from Maedate Shadan in Iwakuraji up to Mine-honsha at the summit, and even today the chief priest of Maedate Shadan also serves as the chief priest of Mine-honsha.

=== Enshrined deities ===
The current enshrined deities are the following two:
* '''{{ill|lt=Izanagi|Q813858|WD=Q813858|lt=Izanagi-no-Ōkami|1=Izanagi}}''' (いざなぎのかみ)
* '''{{ill|lt=Ame-no-Tajikarao|Q849412|WD=Q849412|lt=Ame-no-Tajikarao-no-Kami|1=Ame-no-Tajikarao}}''' (あめのたぢからおのかみ)

=== History ===
The date of foundation is unknown. According to shrine tradition, in the first year of {{ill|lt=Taihō (Japanese era)|Q1346269|WD=Q1346269|lt=Taihō|1=Taihō (era)}} ({{ill|lt=701|Q23496|WD=Q23496|1=701}}), {{ill|lt=Saeki no Ariyori|Q11382848|WD=Q11382848|1=Saeki no Ariyori}}, the son of Saeki no Sukune Ariwaka, a provincial governor of {{ill|lt=Etchū Province|Q906899|WD=Q906899|1=Etchū Province}} said to be a descendant of {{ill|lt=Emperor Keikō|Q329723|WD=Q329723|1=Emperor Keikō}}, was guided by a white hawk to a cave. There he received a divine oracle from Oyama Ōkami stating: “I appear on this mountain to save sentient beings of the defiled world. Sometimes I take the form of a {{ill|lt=hawk|Q846664|WD=Q846664|1=Hawk}}, sometimes that of a {{ill|lt=bear|Q11788|WD=Q11788|1=Bear}}. I have guided you here so that you may open this sacred mountain.” Based on this oracle, the sacred mountain was opened and developed. It is also said that in the third year of Taihō ({{ill|lt=703|Q23500|WD=Q23500|1=703}}), Shakyō Kyōkō enshrined the deity.

Volume 17 of the {{ill|lt=Man’yōshū|Q834321|WD=Q834321|1=Man'yōshū}} includes the “Ode to Tateyama,” composed on April 27 in {{ill|lt=Tenpyō|Q1199879|WD=Q1199879|1=Tenpyō}} 18 ({{ill|lt=746|Q23616|WD=Q23616|1=746}}) by {{ill|lt=Ōtomo no Yakamochi|Q386308|WD=Q386308|1=Ōtomo no Yakamochi}}, who was then the provincial governor of Etchū.

According to official histories, the shrine received promotions in {{ill|lt=court rank|Q11591025|WD=Q11591025|1=Shinkai (divine rank)}} on two occasions:
* {{ill|lt=Nihon Sandai Jitsuroku|Q1052981|WD=Q1052981|1=Nihon Sandai Jitsuroku}}: Entry for September 25, {{ill|lt=Jōgan (Japanese era)|Q1075635|WD=Q1075635|lt=Jōgan|1=Jōgan}} 5 ({{ill|lt=863|Q25052|WD=Q25052|1=863}}), promoted from {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Junior Fifth Rank, Lower Grade|1=UNKNOWN}} to {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Junior Fifth Rank, Upper Grade|1=UNKNOWN}} by {{ill|lt=Emperor Seiwa|Q349243|WD=Q349243|1=Emperor Seiwa}}.
* {{ill|lt=Nihon Kiryaku|Q1053542|WD=Q1053542|1=Nihon Kiryaku}}: Entry for August 22, {{ill|lt=Kanpyō|Q1346292|WD=Q1346292|1=Kanpyō (era)}} 1 ({{ill|lt=889|Q23833|WD=Q23833|1=889}}), promoted from {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Junior Fifth Rank, Upper Grade|1=UNKNOWN}} to {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Junior Fourth Rank, Lower Grade|1=UNKNOWN}} by {{ill|lt=Emperor Uda|Q349267|WD=Q349267|1=Emperor Uda}}.

In {{ill|lt=Enchō (Japanese era)|Q1327274|WD=Q1327274|lt=Enchō|1=Enchō}} 5 ({{ill|lt=927|Q30470|WD=Q30470|1=927}}), it was listed as a minor shrine in the {{ill|lt=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|Q11064932|WD=Q11064932|1=Engishiki Jinmyōchō}}. According to *{{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=The Gods of Japan – Shrines and Sacred Places – Vol. 8 Hokuriku|1=UNKNOWN}}*, texts such as the *Shintōshū* by Angyō-in from the {{ill|lt=Nanboku-chō period (Japan)|Q264012|WD=Q264012|lt=Nanboku-chō period|1=Nanboku-chō period}} and *Nihon Kanoko* describe it as the {{ill|lt=Ichinomiya|Q1656379|WD=Q1656379|1=Ichinomiya}} of {{ill|lt=Etchū Province|Q906899|WD=Q906899|1=Etchū Province}}.

In {{ill|lt=Meiji|Q3125096|WD=Q3125096|1=Meiji era}} 6 ({{ill|lt=1873|Q7757|WD=Q7757|1=1873}}), it became a prefectural shrine, and in {{ill|lt=Shōwa|Q327567|WD=Q327567|1=Shōwa era}} 15 ({{ill|lt=1940|Q18633|WD=Q18633|1=1940}}), it was ranked as a {{ill|lt=Modern shrine ranking system#Government shrines|Q712534|WD=Q712534|lt=Kokuhei-shōsha|1=Modern system of ranked Shinto shrines}}. After World War II, it became a {{ill|lt=Beppyo Shrine|Q10898274|WD=Q10898274|1=Beppyo shrine}} under the {{ill|lt=Association of Shinto Shrines|Q908077|WD=Q908077|1=Association of Shinto Shrines}}.

Since ancient times, Tateyama has been revered nationwide as one of the {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Three Sacred Mountains|1=UNKNOWN}} alongside {{ill|lt=Mount Fuji|Q39231|WD=Q39231|1=Mount Fuji}} and {{ill|lt=Mount Haku|Q2639098|WD=Q2639098|1=Mount Haku}}. Today it is often called “Tateyama climbing” as a tourist or sporting activity, but originally it was “Tateyama worship,” undertaken for the purpose of pilgrimage.

* {{ill|lt=Kenkyū|Q1193933|WD=Q1193933|lt=Kenkyū era|1=Kenkyū}} ({{ill|lt=1190|Q19736|WD=Q19736|1=1190}}–{{ill|lt=1199|Q19752|WD=Q19752|1=1199}}): {{ill|lt=Minamoto no Yoritomo|Q242800|WD=Q242800|1=Minamoto no Yoritomo}} rebuilt the main hall of Maedate Shadan.
* {{ill|lt=Meiō|Q1193841|WD=Q1193841|1=Meiō}} 1 ({{ill|lt=1492|Q6679|WD=Q6679|1=1492}}): {{ill|lt=Ashikaga Yoshitane|Q708527|WD=Q708527|1=Ashikaga Yoshitane}} renovated the main hall of Maedate Shadan.
* {{ill|lt=Tenshō|Q1145058|WD=Q1145058|1=Tenshō (Momoyama period)}} 11 ({{ill|lt=1583|Q6726|WD=Q6726|1=1583}}): {{ill|lt=Sassa Narimasa|Q1186700|WD=Q1186700|1=Sassa Narimasa}} again renovated the main hall of Maedate Shadan.

=== Grounds ===
=== Mine-honsha ===
