==== Overview ====
Until {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Man’en|1=UNKNOWN}} 1 ({{ill|lt=1860|Q7691|WD=Q7691|1=1860}}), successive lords of the {{ill|lt=Kaga Domain|Q903448|WD=Q903448|1=Kaga Domain}} from the {{ill|lt=Maeda clan|Q578033|WD=Q578033|1=Maeda clan}} constructed the shrine buildings. After the {{ill|lt=Meiji|Q3125096|WD=Q3125096|1=Meiji era}} period, construction ceased for some time, but in July of {{ill|lt=Heisei|Q234510|WD=Q234510|1=Heisei era}} 8 ({{ill|lt=1996|Q2070|WD=Q2070|1=1996}}), the shrine building was rebuilt for the first time in 136 years. Mine-honsha is located at the summit of Mount Oyama (3,003 {{ill|lt=meters|Q11573|WD=Q11573|lt=m|1=Metre}}), and winter worship is impossible, so Maedate Shadan was built at the mountain’s foot in Iwakuraji to conduct rituals year-round.

According to *Toyama Prefectural History: Folklore Volume*, in village communities before World War II—particularly in {{ill|lt=Niikawa District|Q11502110|WD=Q11502110|1=Niikawa district}}—it was customary for local youths to undertake the Tateyama pilgrimage as a rite of passage by age 15 at the earliest, or by age 18 at the latest upon coming of age ({{ill|lt=genpuku|Q138321|WD=Q138321|1=Genpuku}}). Those who did not make the pilgrimage were sometimes subject to social criticism. However, since the pilgrimage required a month of abstaining from killing and observing ritual purity beforehand, as well as postponement in cases of mourning, there were instances where it was not undertaken. Pilgrims prepared entirely new white garments, never reusing even a younger brother’s clothing, and even their walking staff was newly made. Around {{ill|lt=1907|Q2048|WD=Q2048|1=1907}}, clothing colors and styles became freer, but before that, white attire was the norm. This custom has been passed down through school climbing activities, and even as of {{ill|lt=2009|Q1996|WD=Q1996|1=2009}}, some elementary schools in Toyama Prefecture still include Tateyama climbing as an annual event.

Pilgrims would pick up one to three stones along riverbeds on the way and offer them beside Mine-honsha, laying them in the forecourt. According to local tradition recorded in *Toyama Prefectural History: Folklore Volume*, this practice stemmed from a tale that when Tateyama and {{ill|lt=Mount Haku|Q2639098|WD=Q2639098|1=Mount Haku}} in {{ill|lt=Kaga Province|Q907469|WD=Q907469|lt=Kaga|1=Kaga Province}} compared heights, Tateyama was lower by the thickness of a horse’s straw sandal, so stones were carried up to make it taller. The same source also considers other examples—such as the {{ill|lt=“Oshiraishi-mochi” ritual|Q17225061|WD=Q17225061|1=Oshiraishimochi}} at {{ill|lt=Ise Grand Shrine|Q687168|WD=Q687168|1=Ise Grand Shrine}}, the river stones laid in the sacred court of Ashikuraji Wakamiya ({{ill|lt=#Chūgū Kiganden|UNKNOWN|lt=Chūgū Kiganden}}), and the offering of three clean beach stones called “Okesoku” at graves during {{ill|lt=Obon|Q1154888|WD=Q1154888|1=Obon}} in {{ill|lt=Asahi Town (Toyama)|Q1204470|WD=Q1204470|1=Asahi, Toyama}}—and suggests that, while differing from ancestor worship, the stones may have been offerings imbued with divine or magical power to the god of Tateyama.

According to *Toyama Prefectural History: Folklore Volume*, pilgrims would buy red long banners as souvenirs when descending the mountain, or descend with such banners raised. As of {{ill|lt=2009|Q1996|WD=Q1996|1=2009}}, pilgrims who receive prayers for safe climbing in front of the Mine-honsha shrine building are given a red talisman reading “Oyama Shrine at the Summit of Tateyama,” which they attach to white wooden kongō staffs, trekking poles, or {{ill|lt=backpacks|Q5843|WD=Q5843|1=Backpack}} while descending.

* Subsidiary and branch shrines
* Other facilities: Amulet office
* Access: About a 2-hour walk from {{ill|lt=Murodō Terminal|Q6938821|WD=Q6938821|1=Murodō Station}} on the {{ill|lt=Tateyama Kurobe Alpine Route|Q6425988|WD=Q6425988|1=Tateyama Kurobe Alpine Route}}

