==== Overview ====
The Maedate Shrine Platform is a syncretic Shinto-Buddhist ({{ill|lt=Shinbutsu-shūgō|Q2279088|WD=Q2279088|1=Shinbutsu-shūgō}}) site that originated from Tateyama-dera Temple (Iwakuraji), founded in the early Heian period. It served as the center of {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Tateyama worship|1=UNKNOWN}} at Iwakuraji.

The site stands directly in front of a cave where the founder {{ill|lt=Saeki no Ariyori|Q11382848|WD=Q11382848|1=Saeki no Ariyori}} was guided by a white hawk, a manifestation of {{ill|lt=Tateyama Gongen|Q11597837|WD=Q11597837|1=Tateyama Gongen}}, marking the beginning of the legend of Tateyama’s sacred opening. Revered by warriors and court nobles alike, offerings to Tateyama Gongen were traditionally dedicated here.

Those entering {{ill|lt=Mount Tateyama|Q2977103|WD=Q2977103|1=Mount Tate}} purified bodily defilement and sins through hot-water purification rites and prayed for safety along the journey. Even today, temple lodgings, old mountain trails, and stone Buddhas remain scattered nearby. Tateyama mandalas are still preserved in the lodgings, which once spread Tateyama worship to followers nationwide.

Beside the site flows the {{ill|lt=Jōganji River|Q994562|WD=Q994562|1=Jōganji River}}, known as Japan’s most violent river ({{ill|lt=raging river|Q11514995|WD=Q11514995|1=raging river}}). Repeated flood damage reduced the complex to its present scale, but numerous shrines downstream enshrine sacred objects said to have been carried away from this site, suggesting that it was once far larger.

The buildings originally constructed by {{ill|lt=Saeki no Ariyori|Q11382848|WD=Q11382848|1=Saeki no Ariyori}} survive only in ancient documents. The present structures were restored and rebuilt by {{ill|lt=Minamoto no Yoritomo|Q242800|WD=Q242800|1=Minamoto no Yoritomo}} and later repaired by {{ill|lt=Ashikaga Yoshiki|Q242793|WD=Q242793|1=Lanuvio}}. As a result, it is a rare shrine whose buildings do not bear the {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Chrysanthemum Crest|1=UNKNOWN}}.

Because it stands at the entrance to {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Tateyama worship|1=UNKNOWN}}, the shrine is characterized by having torii gates (front torii and east torii) and shrine gates (front shrine gate and east shrine gate) on both sides of the main hall.

* Main hall architectural style: Nagare-zukuri with a five-bay frontage and one-bay porch, roofed with cypress bark (the largest main hall of its type in Hokuriku)
* Subsidiary and branch shrines: Inari Shrine, Tachi-o Shrine, Hachiman Shrine
* Former subsidiary structures: Great Lecture Hall, Goma Hall, Worship Hall, Hōrin Hall, Suijin Shrine, Inari Hall, Aizen Shrine, Kannon Hall, Jizō Hall, Wakamiya Shrine Hall, and others
* Other facilities: Monument inscribed with Emperor {{ill|lt=Shōwa|Q34479|WD=Q34479|1=Hirohito}}’s poem “{{ill|lt=Tateyama no Uta|Q21015447|WD=Q21015447|1=Tateyama no Uta}},” former Saikan Hall
* Historical documents: 481 items
* Access: About 10 minutes on foot from {{ill|lt=Iwakuraji Station|Q6100451|WD=Q6100451|1=Iwakuraji Station}} on the {{ill|lt=Toyama Chihō Railway Tateyama Line|Q7830575|WD=Q7830575|1=Toyama Chihō Railway Tateyama Line}}, or about 15 minutes on foot from {{ill|lt=Ōkawadera Station|Q5209603|WD=Q5209603|1=Daisenji Station}} on the {{ill|lt=Toyama Chihō Railway Kamitaki Line|Q7830572|WD=Q7830572|1=Toyama Chihō Railway Kamidaki Line}}

